<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ginkaku-ji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1482">1482</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Silver Pavilion, Jishō-ji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ginkaku-ji, the Temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> Pavilion, is a Zen temple in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, built from 1482 as the retirement villa of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> Ashikaga Yoshimasa. Despite its name, the pavilion was never covered in silver. The villa was a centre of Higashiyama culture, which shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a>, ink painting and garden design, and it became a temple after Yoshimasa's death in 1490.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ginkaku-ji.jpg" alt="Ginkaku-ji" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oilstreet · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ginkakuji_Kyoto03-r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ginkaku-ji, officially named Jishō-ji, 'Temple of Shining Mercy', is a Zen temple in the Sakyo ward of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and one of the constructions that represent the Higashiyama culture of the Muromachi period. Ashikaga Yoshimasa planned a retirement villa and gardens as early as 1460, a pleasure villa where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> could rest from their administrative duties. Work on its main structure, the two-storied Kannon-den, or Kannon hall, began on 21 February 1482. Yoshimasa wanted it to emulate the golden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinkaku-ji">Kinkaku-ji</a>, commissioned by his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a>. Like Kinkaku-ji, it was built as a place of rest and solitude for the shogun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Kannon-den is popularly known as Ginkaku, the Silver Pavilion, because of the initial plans to cover its exterior in silver foil. That nickname dates only from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> (1600 to 1868). Construction was halted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a>, and the silver overlay was delayed so long that it was never applied before Yoshimasa's death. The present appearance of the building is understood to be the same as when Yoshimasa last saw it, and this unfinished look is taken to illustrate the quality of wabi-sabi.</p><p>According to Ichito Ishida and Delmer M. Brown, the building was planned in relation to its surroundings: openings in the south-east corner of the ground floor look across a pond to where the moon rises between the peaks of Higashiyama, and the north-east room was planned as a library because a lake reflected enough light to read by.</p><p>The property has wooded grounds covered with a variety of mosses. The garden was supposedly designed by the landscape artist Sōami. Its sand garden is particularly well known, and a carefully formed pile of sand, said to symbolise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-fuji">Mount Fuji</a>, is an essential element. Because the garden was financed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> as a retreat for aesthetic enjoyment, rather than designed by and for monks practising zazen, it does not represent Zen culture entirely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Yoshimasa inspired a new outpouring of traditional culture, known as Higashiyama Bunka, the culture of the Eastern Mountain. It is said that, having retired to the villa, he sat in the pavilion contemplating the gardens while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> worsened and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> burned. In 1485 he became a Zen Buddhist monk, and after his death on 27 January 1490 the villa became a Buddhist temple complex, renamed Jishō-ji after his Buddhist name.</p><p>The temple is associated with the Shōkoku-ji branch of Rinzai Zen. After extensive restoration begun in February 2008 the garden and temple complex are open to the public. No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> foil has been used, and it was decided not to refinish the lacquer to its original state; the lacquer was the source of the original silver appearance, through the reflection of the pond on its surface.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ginkaku-ji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culture flourished under shogunal patronage: Zeami's Noh, Sesshu's ink painting, Zen temples and the <b>Silver Pavilion</b> of Yoshimasa.</blockquote><a href="/state/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate</a></li></ul></section>
